@* Says where a value came from, on hover. "593.6 MB" is only useful once you know it is the working set,
   and "mailto:someone" only once you know which setting produced it - so every value that is read from
   somewhere rather than typed into the markup names its source here.

   Off unless the period on the Temporary settings page is running, and then it costs one span and one
   attribute per value. The tooltip itself is CSS: this project has no JavaScript, and a title attribute
   cannot be styled, does not appear on a touch screen, and waits a second before showing. *@

@inject ITechnicalTooltips Tooltips

@if (Source is { Length: > 0 } source && Tooltips.IsEnabled)
{
    <span class="tech" tabindex="0" data-tech="@source">@ChildContent</span>
}
else
{
    @ChildContent
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Where the value comes from. Nothing is rendered when it is empty.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>One grammar, and every annotation in the admin UI follows it</b> - a tooltip that explains
    /// itself in prose on one page and names a column on the next is worse than none, because the reader
    /// cannot tell which they will get. It is the technical name of the thing and nothing else:
    /// <list type="bullet">
    /// <item>a runtime figure is its metric name - <c>Working Set (MB)</c>,
    /// <c>GC Collections Gen0/Gen1/Gen2</c>;</item>
    /// <item>a setting is its key path under the file it lives in - <c>config.json
    /// Dlna.Server.ManufacturerUrl</c>;</item>
    /// <item>an indexed value is its table and column - <c>database Files.SizeInBytes (MB)</c>.</item>
    /// </list>
    /// A unit or rendering goes in brackets at the end (<c>(MB)</c>, <c>(UTC)</c>, <c>(%)</c>). There is
    /// no explanatory clause after a dash or a comma: the page's own labels and hints are where prose
    /// belongs.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Source { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The value itself.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
